Hardware Setup

First, let's define the pin connections:

TB6612FNG Connections (for 4 motors):

    Motor 1 (Front Left): AIN1, AIN2, PWMA

    Motor 2 (Front Right): BIN1, BIN2, PWMB

    Motor 3 (Rear Left): AIN1, AIN2, PWMA (second driver)

    Motor 4 (Rear Right): BIN1, BIN2, PWMB (second driver)

Pin Configuration:

    Adjust the pin numbers in the code according to your actual wiring:

    MotorDriver(17, 18, 4) means IN1=GPIO17, IN2=GPIO18, PWM=GPIO4

    Update encoder pins similarly

Control Scheme

    Left Stick:

        Up/Down: Forward/Backward

        Left/Right: Strafe Left/Right

    Right Stick:

        Left/Right: Rotate Counterclockwise/Clockwise

    Xbox Button: Exit program

Features

    Mecanum Kinematics: Proper wheel speed calculations for omnidirectional movement

    Encoder Support: Quadrature encoder reading for future closed-loop control

    Deadzone Handling: Prevents drift from joystick imperfections

    Smooth Control: Normalized speed calculations

    Safety Features: Clean shutdown on exit

Important Notes

    Power Management: Your 12V LiFePO4 battery and USB PD module should provide adequate power

    Motor Connections: Ensure motors are wired correctly for the proper rotation directions

    Encoder Setup: You may need to adjust encoder pin assignments and counting logic

    Safety: Test in a safe area first and consider adding emergency stop functionality

This code provides a solid foundation that you can extend with features like PID control, autonomous navigation, or additional controller functions!


1. OLED Face Process

    Independent process for facial animations

    Random expression changes with weighted probabilities

    Command-driven expressions with priority system

2. Expressions Library

    8 Different Expressions: Neutral, Happy, Sad, Angry, Surprised, Blink, Sleepy, Wink

    Realistic Eye Animations: Open, closed, winking, angry, sad

    Mouth Animations: Smile, frown, neutral, surprised

3. Smart Expression Triggers

    Movement-based: Happy when moving, neutral when stopped

    Button-triggered: Special expressions for controller buttons

    Emergency expressions: Surprised for emergency stops

    Random animations: Natural-looking random blinks and expressions

4. Hardware Integration

    Supports multiple I2C addresses (default: 0x3C, 0x3D, 0x3E)

    Automatic display detection and fallback

    Clean shutdown with display clearing

Installation Requirements:
bash

sudo apt update
sudo apt install python3-pip python3-pygame python3-pil
pip3 install RPi.GPIO pygame luma.oled pillow

I2C Setup:
bash

sudo raspi-config
# Enable I2C in Interface Options
sudo i2cdetect -y 1  # Check OLED addresses

Usage:

    Automatic: Expressions change randomly

    Controller Buttons:

        A: Happy

        B: Surprised

        X: Wink left

        Y: Wink right

    Movement: Auto-happy when moving

    Emergency: Surprised face on emergency stop

The OLED face adds personality to your robot and provides visual feedback about the robot's state!